{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>After she has been defiled</b> — to include the suspected wife who secluded herself [with another man]; this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Deuteronomy 24:4). Its meaning is: a woman who committed adultery under her husband. But the suspected wife who merely secluded herself — since it is not known that she is defiled, he does not transgress this prohibition on her account. And so it is explicitly stated in the first chapter of Yevamot (11a): What is the meaning of וְנִסְתְּרָה (\"and she secluded herself\")? She had relations. And why does Scripture call her נִסְתְּרָה (\"one who secluded herself\")? It employs a euphemistic expression. And they asked: She had relations openly — טֻמְאָה (defilement) is written concerning her (Numbers 5:13), and [concerning one who] secluded herself and was defiled — to derive a prohibition concerning her. If so, let Scripture say: her first husband, who sent her away, may not take her back to be his wife, and after she has been defiled. And behold, these are two prohibitions: one warning against one who takes back his divorcee after she has married another, and one warning against one who takes back his wife who was defiled through adultery. And according to the plain sense, אַחֲרֵי אֲשֶׁר הֻטַּמָּאָה (\"after she has been defiled\") — to the first husband — alludes that once another man has known her, she is considered defiled to him. And these are commandments that are novel regarding divorce and return. And the reason for this prohibition is so that they not exchange their wives with one another — he would write her a bill of divorce in the evening and in the morning she returns to him. And this is the reason for וְלֹא תַחֲטִיא אֶת הָאָרֶץ (\"and you shall not bring sin upon the land\"), for this is a cause of great sins. And in the Sifrei (§243): וְלֹא תַחֲטִיא אֶת הָאָרֶץ — to warn the court concerning this."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 24, :notes {0 "The Sifrei is cited by parashah name (תצא) and number (קלו = 236 in some editions, 243 in others); the manuscript abbreviation is resolved to Sifrei Devarim 243 based on standard references."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:15:29.650372201Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}